a nested list is indented under the item it belongs to

§2.7's missing half. A sub-bullet rendered in exactly its parent's column: the walk
that collects a list's items **recursed into nested lists and threw the depth away**,
pushing every level into one flat vector, and the renderer had a single indent step.

**The parse already had the tree — this is a seam, not a parse.** `collect_items` is
given the `list` children of each item and calls itself on them; what it did not do was
record where those items came from. So the fix is to carry one more vector alongside
`items`, and the question is only what number goes in it.

## The number comes from the source, not the recursion depth

`marker_indent` reads the **column the marker was actually written at**, from the marker
node's byte offset and the source that is right there, then rounds it down to an even
number and caps it at 8. All three of those are leticl's constants and each earns its
place:

- **From the source** rather than a depth counter, because the two disagree for exactly
  the lists people write: a `- ` sub-list is written at two spaces and a `1. ` one at
  three or four, and the model's own indentation is the fact its prose refers to.
- **Rounded to even** so those two land on the *same* step. A head that indented by the
  raw column would put a bullet sub-list and an ordered sub-list a column apart for no
  reason a reader could name.
- **Capped at 8** so a deeply nested list cannot walk off the width it is rendered at.
  Six levels deep flattens the last two onto 8 rather than continuing 10 and 12, and
  that is asserted rather than assumed.
- **Two columns per step**, which is the step `card::REASONING_RAIL_WIDTH` and
  `App::GUTTER` already use, so the page keeps one idea of what one level of structure
  costs instead of three unrelated indents.

The nest is added **inside** the marker-width arithmetic rather than prepended to the
output row, so a wrapped sub-item's continuation lines line up under its own text and not
under its parent's — the same reason the `pad` there is measured in columns rather than
bytes.

## The ordering conflict, ruled — and this head's description was stale

§2.7 lists a **CONFLICT** on ordered numbering: this head rendering `1. 1. 1.` for a
loose list, *"one block per item, each with its own written `start`"*, against leticl's
`1. 2. 3.`.

**That description is out of date, and the check is one line of code**: `lex("1. first\n\n2.
second\n\n3. third\n\n")` produces **one** block with three items, not three blocks —
the grammar keeps a loose list together, which is the same fix that put `start + i` in
the renderer. So this head numbers `1. 2. 3.` too, and the conflict as stated does not
exist.

What is worth ruling is the case the two heads *could* still differ on: a model that
writes `1.` for every item, which markdown permits and models do. **Numbering up is
right**, because the repeated `1.` is an idiom rather than a claim about the number —
every markdown renderer on this box shows `1. 2. 3.` for that input, so a head echoing
`1. 1. 1.` shows something no other reader of the same message sees. And **the first
item's written number is kept**, so a list the model numbered from seven stays at seven:
the rule is "count from what was written", not "renumber", and a model phrasing its own
list is not quietly corrected. Both are pinned by
`a_loose_ordered_list_counts_up_rather_than_echoing_a_repeated_marker`.

## Tests

- `a_nested_list_is_indented_under_the_item_it_belongs_to` — columns `0, 2, 4, 0` for
  three levels plus a sibling; a `1. ` sub-list at three spaces and at four landing on
  the **same** step as a `- ` one at two; and six levels cramming onto `0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 8`
  so the cap is exercised rather than asserted.
- `a_loose_ordered_list_counts_up_rather_than_echoing_a_repeated_marker` — the ruling
  above, at the render level, plus `7. 8.` staying `7. 8.`.
- The existing `a_loose_ordered_list_keeps_the_numbers_it_was_written_with` is unchanged
  and still passes, which is the evidence for the stale-description claim: it asserts one
  block with three items, and it did so before this commit.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **311 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (309 + 2).
`cargo test -p letibot-ui`: **101 passed, 0 failed**.

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run — it writes
a temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only
the operator lifts. `letibot-harnessd`, `-sessionlog`, `-tokencore` and `-turn` are still
unrun since `65725ce`, for the lock reasons the last two commits record.

## Not in this commit

**The rest of §6's missing-in-A list**, which is the next block of work and is mostly
small: `/todos`, `/subagents`, `/peek`, `/resume`, `/promote`, unknown verbs forwarded to
the daemon rather than refused against an allowlist, `q` closing any pane, `ctrl-o` into
a subagent, clicks reaching five panes instead of two, SGR mouse motion decoded, the
composer draft preserved across a history walk, the truthful paste line count (this head
reports 301 lines for a 300-line paste), and the tail-origin scroll sign for the peek and
job-output panes.

**A defect found while reading the lexer, not fixed here**: the settled half of a list
takes its indent from the source column, but `IncrementalMarkdown` freezes a prefix once
it is lexed — so an item that is indented *after* it has already been rendered into the
frozen half would keep its old column. It needs a fence-like guard in `cut_point` if it
is real, and it is recorded rather than guessed at.
